  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "The short answer",
      "items": [
        "The route calls transitionWorkflow, which does everything in one transaction: it checks the actor and phase, applies a compare-and-set on the ticket's workflow version, queues emails and callbacks, and appends an audit block",
        "The live event is published only after commit"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "slate",
      "title": "Not shown here",
      "items": [
        "The guards call (the route performs it before the service) and the individual action rules: claim_review, route, offer, pickup, accept, decline, release and return_to_service_desk, which differ only in their phase and role checks",
        "Connected but out of scope: Auth.js sessions, API-key integrator, Microsoft Teams, Context and guards, API key service, Unified intake"
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "rose",
      "title": "What it guarantees",
      "items": [
        "Workflow transitions are compare-and-set on workflowVersion, so two staff acting at once cannot both win: the loser gets 'Ticket changed. Refresh and try again.'",
        "Each audit block hashes its predecessor and is written in the same transaction as the change it records: a verify call can recompute the chain and point to the first altered block"
      ]
    }
  ]
